<h2 id="platforms">Into trading platforms</h2>
<p>
    Trading platforms such as NinjaTrader, MetaTrader and TradeStation each import history in their own way. DbnSuite.Fetch
    writes one platform's format natively, NinjaTrader 8's, and plain CSV for the rest. It does not write
    MetaTrader's or TradeStation's formats, and their import steps are not covered here.
</p>
<h3 id="ninjatrader">NinjaTrader 8</h3>
<p>
    <code>--format nt8</code> writes NinjaTrader's import text: bars from <code>--bars</code>, or ticks from
    <code>--as trades</code>. Bars are stamped with their end; a bar of a day or longer has the date alone, whatever the
    session. A contract's file is named for NinjaTrader's instrument, such as <code>ES 12-26.Last.txt</code>, which is
    how NinjaTrader knows where to put it. NinjaTrader's layout is fixed, so <code>--columns</code> is refused. Name a
    contract or a continuous series, since a root's contracts would be mixed into one file, and the whole range goes in
    one file.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@Nt8" />
<ol class="steps-list">
    <li>In NinjaTrader 8: <strong>Control Center → Tools → Import → Historical Data</strong>.</li>
    <li>Choose the format with <strong>end of bar</strong> timestamps, and set the time zone to the one you exported in (Chicago unless you passed <code>--tz</code>).</li>
    <li>Add the file and import. For a continuous series, rename the file to the instrument you want it in first.</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="other-platforms">Other platforms</h3>
<p>
    Export bars as CSV, keep only the columns your platform's importer asks for, in its order, and write times in the
    zone it expects. Then follow the platform's own import instructions.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@PlatformCsv" />

<h2 id="rolls">Rolls</h2>
<p>
    <code>dbnf rolls</code> lists a continuous series' rolls: the first trade date the new contract is used, the
    contracts, and the settlement gap that <code>--adjust</code> uses. A roll decided by Friday's volume shows Monday,
    since Sunday's open belongs to Monday's trade date. Exchange holidays are not known, so one is not skipped. A roll
    with no settlement shows dashes. It takes the same dates as <code>export</code> (<code>--from</code>/<code>--to</code>,
    <code>--last</code> or <code>--since</code>), and <code>--roll-days</code> changes a <code>.c.</code> series' rule.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="dbnf rolls ES.v.0 --since 2025-01-01" />

<h2 id="tapes">Tapes</h2>
<p>
    <code>dbnf tape</code> builds compact files for backtesting from the archive, and proves them against it. See
    <a href="docs/dbntape">DbnTape</a>.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@TapeBuild" />

<h2 id="mcp">MCP server</h2>
<p>
    <code>dbnf mcp</code> runs dbnf as a <a href="@Links.Mcp">Model Context Protocol</a> server
    on standard input and output, so Claude Code and other AI agents can ask about your archive. It reads the archive
    every other command reads: the login, or <code>--profile</code>, <code>--archive</code>, the <code>DBNF_</code>
    variables and <code>--cache</code>. Log in first; nothing the server offers logs in or out, touches a key,
    deletes anything, builds tapes or spends money, and no result or error ever shows a key.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Add it to Claude Code" Text="@McpAdd" />
<p>
    By default it offers only facts about the archive. Two options add more, and each is your choice to make:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong><code>--allow-data</code></strong> adds the tools that return market data. A tool's result goes to the
        AI model's provider, which is passing Databento data to a third party, so turn this on only if your Databento
        agreement allows it. Without it, those tools aren't offered at all.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong><code>--out &lt;folder&gt;</code></strong> adds the tools that write files, only inside that folder. A path
        that would leave it, rooted or with <code>..</code>, is refused. They return paths, row counts and sizes, never
        the data. Each first weighs what it would read or copy, and refuses more than <code>--max-gb</code> (2 GB by
        default) unless the call confirms, which the agent should only do once you agree. Each also takes
        <code>dry_run</code>, to only weigh the job, and <code>folder</code>, a subfolder of <code>--out</code>.
    </li>
</ul>
<CodeBlock Title="Or, with more tools" Text="@McpMore" />
<p>
    Tapes usually live in a tape store with a login of their own: add a second server with that
    <code>--profile</code>, or give <code>--tapes</code> a folder of tapes. <code>--tapes</code> is read by
    <code>tape_catalog</code> and <code>tape_info</code>; <code>tape_pull</code> copies from the server's own login,
    which must be the tape store's.
</p>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Offered</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>archive_status</code></td><td>always</td><td>Which archive, whether the login reaches it, the clock, and the cache and its free space, as <code>doctor</code> checks them.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>list_series</code></td><td>always</td><td>Roots and schemas with days held, days with no data, size, first and last day, as <code>ls</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>coverage</code></td><td>always</td><td>A root and schema's days in a range: how many hold a file, the days with no data, and the days never fetched.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>contracts</code></td><td>always</td><td>A root's contracts with expiry and tick size.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>rolls</code></td><td>always</td><td>A continuous series' rolls, with settlements and gaps.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>trading_calendar</code></td><td>always</td><td>Sessions over a range of trade dates: open and close, early closes, holidays, halts and RTH, for the US equity index futures.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tape_catalog</code>, <code>tape_info</code></td><td>always</td><td>Which trade dates have tapes, the contract each holds, and a tape's header. They read catalogs; they never build one.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>volume_by_day</code></td><td>always</td><td>Each trade date's total volume and most traded contract: the <code>ohlcv-1s</code> bars of outright contracts, summed. Daily totals only.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>show_records</code></td><td><code>--allow-data</code></td><td>Up to 200 of a day's records, as <code>show</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>bars</code></td><td><code>--allow-data</code></td><td>Up to 200 OHLCV bars of one trade date, built as <code>export --bars</code> builds them.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>export</code></td><td><code>--out</code></td><td>Most of <code>export</code>'s options, into the folder; <code>--hours</code> is always Chicago time.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>pull</code></td><td><code>--out</code></td><td>As <code>pull --out</code>, into the folder.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tape_pull</code></td><td><code>--out</code></td><td>As <code>tape pull</code>, into the folder, but never removing a tape.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    Standard output belongs to the protocol while it serves, and the server ends when the agent closes it. Long
    exports and pulls report progress, and stop if the agent cancels them.
</p>

<h2 id="cache">The cache</h2>
<p>
    Raw files are cached in <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\DbnFetch\cache</code> on Windows and
    <code>~/.cache/DbnFetch</code> on macOS and Linux (<code>$XDG_CACHE_HOME/DbnFetch</code> on Linux when that is set), in a folder for each login, so a second export of the same days downloads nothing. A file that
    changes in the archive is downloaded again. <code>--cache</code> on any command uses another folder.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><code>dbnf cache</code> shows what it holds.</li>
    <li><code>dbnf cache clear</code> deletes all of it; <code>dbnf cache clear mbo</code> one schema; <code>dbnf cache clear mbo MNQ</code> one schema of one root.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    A cached file that is damaged stops the command with an error naming it and the command that mends it, such as
    <code>dbnf pull mbo MNQ --from 2026-10-29 --force</code>, which downloads it again. In a folder archive, copy a good
    file over it. If <code>profiles.json</code> or <code>secrets.json</code> is damaged, the error names it; run
    <code>dbnf login</code> again, which replaces it and keeps the old file beside it, ending <code>.damaged</code>.
</p>

<h2 id="unattended">Running unattended</h2>
<p>
    On a server, or anywhere dbnf runs on a schedule, give it the archive through the <code>DBNF_</code> variables
    instead of a login, and keep them in a file only you can read. This one points at a tape Space, for
    <code>tape pull</code>; for the archive itself, use its bucket, <code>dbn</code>. A systemd user timer then runs it every morning,
    after the night's data has arrived; <code>loginctl enable-linger</code> lets it run while you're logged out.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="~/.config/dbnf.env (chmod 600)" Text="@UnattendedEnv" />
<CodeBlock Title="~/.config/systemd/user/dbnf-pull.service" Text="@UnattendedService" />
<CodeBlock Title="~/.config/systemd/user/dbnf-pull.timer" Text="@UnattendedTimer" />
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@UnattendedEnable" />
<p>
    A tape already there is skipped, so a fixed <code>--from</code> only fetches what's new. dbnf needs no
    terminal, and its <a href="docs/dbnfetch#exit">exit code</a> says how it went, so a failed run
    shows in <code>systemctl --user status dbnf-pull</code> and <code>journalctl --user -u dbnf-pull</code>. With
    cron instead, load the same file first: <code>set -a; . ~/.config/dbnf.env; set +a; dbnf …</code>.
</p>

<h2 id="exit">Exit codes</h2>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>It worked.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The work failed: the archive refused or couldn't be reached, a file is damaged, a roll has no settlement. <code>dbnf doctor</code> also exits 1 if a check fails.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>A usage mistake: an option or argument dbnf can't parse, a value it can't use, a request it refuses, or no login.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>130</code></td><td>Stopped with Ctrl+C, in a command that reads the archive.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="reference">Option reference</h2>
<p><code>dbnf --help</code> and <code>dbnf &lt;command&gt; --help</code> print every option. These work on every command:</p>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>--profile</code></td><td><code>default</code></td><td>Which saved login to use.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--archive</code></td><td>the login's</td><td>Read a folder instead.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>--cache</code></td><td>see above</td><td>Where raw files are kept once downloaded.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>login</code>, <code>logout</code></td><td>Save or forget the archive's address and your key.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>doctor</code></td><td>Check the login, the archive, the clock and the cache.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ls</code></td><td>List series: schema, root, days, size, first and last day.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>coverage &lt;root&gt;</code></td><td>One root day by day.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>contracts &lt;root&gt;</code></td><td>Contracts with expiry and tick size.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>show &lt;schema&gt; &lt;symbol&gt; &lt;day&gt;</code></td><td>Print one UTC day's records.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>pull &lt;schemas&gt; &lt;roots&gt;</code></td><td>Download raw files.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>export &lt;schema&gt; &lt;symbol&gt;</code></td><td>Write records or bars for trade dates.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>rolls &lt;series&gt;</code></td><td>List a continuous series' rolls.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tape</code></td><td>Build, inspect and prove tapes: <code>catalog</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>verify</code>, <code>pull</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>schema</code>, <code>bench</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>cache</code>, <code>cache clear</code></td><td>Show or clear the local cache.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>mcp</code></td><td>Serve the archive to AI agents over the <a href="docs/dbnfetch#mcp">Model Context Protocol</a>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

